  "track.coroutines.m.exception.val.delivered": "delivered",
  "track.coroutines.m.exception.val.held": "being held",
  "track.coroutines.m.exception.s.setup.title": "What does this setup contain?",
  "track.coroutines.m.exception.s.setup.explain": "The scope is a `coroutineScope`: both children are `{builder}` and are children of the scope Job. A failure of one child is handled through the parent–child relation.",
  "track.coroutines.m.exception.s.setup.explainSup": "The scope is a `supervisorScope`: both children are `{builder}`; a failure or cancellation of one does not spread to the scope or the other child.",
  "track.coroutines.m.exception.s.throw.title": "{who} throws",
  "track.coroutines.m.exception.s.throw.explainLaunch": "The `launch` child throws. `launch` treats it as an uncaught exception.",
  "track.coroutines.m.exception.s.throw.explainAsync": "The `async` child throws. `async` keeps the exception in the `Deferred` and lets the caller receive it through `await`.",
  "track.coroutines.m.exception.s.propagate.title": "How does the failure go up to the parent?",
  "track.coroutines.m.exception.s.propagate.explain": "With a regular Job, the failure of {who} fails the scope and cancels {other}. This is structured concurrency.",
  "track.coroutines.m.exception.s.contain.title": "Why do the scope and the other child stay alive?",
  "track.coroutines.m.exception.s.contain.explain": "`supervisorScope` does not spread the failure of {who} to the scope or to {other}; {other} keeps running.",
  "track.coroutines.m.exception.s.deliver.handler.title": "`CoroutineExceptionHandler` receives the error",
  "track.coroutines.m.exception.s.deliver.handler.explain": "The error reaches the handler, which is for logging or showing an error. The handler runs after the coroutine has already completed with the exception, so it cannot recover.",
  "track.coroutines.m.exception.s.deliver.uncaught.title": "Nobody handles the error",
  "track.coroutines.m.exception.s.deliver.uncaught.explain": "No handler takes effect, so the exception goes to the default uncaught handler (typically printed to the log) .",
  "track.coroutines.m.exception.s.deliver.caughtScope.title": "`try/catch` around the scope catches it",
  "track.coroutines.m.exception.s.deliver.caughtScope.explain": "The scope rethrows the exception to its caller, so a `try/catch` around it catches it. The children had already been cancelled.",
  "track.coroutines.m.exception.s.deliver.caughtAwait.title": "`try/catch` around `await()` catches it",
  "track.coroutines.m.exception.s.deliver.caughtAwait.explain": "`await()` rethrows the exception kept in the `Deferred`, so a `try/catch` around it catches it.",
  "track.coroutines.m.exception.s.deliver.held.title": "The error sits quietly in the `Deferred`",
  "track.coroutines.m.exception.s.deliver.held.explain": "Nobody calls `await()`, so the exception only sits in the `Deferred`: no log, no crash, and nobody knows.",
  "track.coroutines.m.exception.s.note.scopeStillFails.title": "Does catching at `await()` save the scope?",
  "track.coroutines.m.exception.s.note.scopeStillFails.explain": "No. The failure already cancelled the scope and its children when the child threw, so catching at `await()` only handles it locally; the scope still fails.",
  "track.coroutines.m.exception.s.note.childIgnored.title": "Why is a handler on the child useless here?",
  "track.coroutines.m.exception.s.note.childIgnored.explain": "With a regular Job the child failure goes to its parent instead of the child handler, so a handler placed on the child has no effect. Put the handler at the root.",
  "track.coroutines.m.exception.s.note.scopeNoThrow.title": "Why does `try/catch` around the scope catch nothing?",
  "track.coroutines.m.exception.s.note.scopeNoThrow.explain": "`supervisorScope` does not fail because a `launch` child failed, so no exception is rethrown to the caller.",
  "track.coroutines.m.exception.s.note.heldNote.title": "How do you learn about an `async` failure?",
  "track.coroutines.m.exception.s.note.heldNote.explain": "Call `await()` (inside `try/catch` if you want to handle it) or consume the `Deferred` result; otherwise the failure is missed.",
  "track.coroutines.m.exception.ev.launch": "{builder} launched",
