  "track.coroutines.m.hotcold.co.cold": "A cold flow starts producing when collected, and each collector triggers its own run; a hot flow emits independently of its collectors. [[src:kt-flow]]",
  "track.coroutines.m.hotcold.co.state": "StateFlow is a state holder: it emits the current and new state updates to its collectors, and the current value can be read through `value`. [[src:and-stateflow]]",
  "track.coroutines.m.hotcold.co.shared": "The example in the documentation uses `shareIn(..., replay = 1)` to replay the last value to a new collector. [[src:and-stateflow]]",
  "track.coroutines.m.hotcold.co.channel": "A Channel is conceptually close to a `BlockingQueue` but `send`/`receive` suspend; send and receive from several coroutines are served in first-in first-out order, and the first coroutine to call `receive` gets the element. A Channel is not a broadcast Flow. [[src:kt-channels]]",
  "track.coroutines.m.lifecycle.title": "Screen lifecycle and where a Flow is collected",
  "track.coroutines.m.lifecycle.overview": "A Flow emits values continuously. You choose to collect it with `repeatOnLifecycle(STARTED)`, directly in the screen scope, or run it in `viewModelScope`, then send the screen to the background, bring it back, or close it.",
  "track.coroutines.m.lifecycle.wrapup": "`repeatOnLifecycle(STARTED)` cancels at STOPPED and restarts at STARTED, so it collects nothing while the screen is hidden. Collecting directly in the screen scope keeps running while STOPPED. `viewModelScope` stops only when the ViewModel is cleared.",
  "track.coroutines.m.lifecycle.simp.1": "The screen has only three states, STARTED, STOPPED and DESTROYED; RESUMED, rotation and process death are not simulated. The number of values received while hidden (3 per step) is illustrative.",
  "track.coroutines.m.lifecycle.simp.2": "The \"screen scope\" is described conceptually as cancelled when the screen is destroyed; the model does not rely on a specific scope API.",
  "track.coroutines.m.lifecycle.ctl.hint": "The model recomputes the sequence from the start; you can add up to 6 actions.",
