  "track.coroutines.m.race.s.read.explain": "{who} reads the current counter value ({v}) into a local variable.",
  "track.coroutines.m.race.ev.read": "{who} reads the counter",
  "track.coroutines.m.race.s.write.title": "{who} writes the new value",
  "track.coroutines.m.race.s.write.explain": "{who} writes the value plus 1; the counter becomes {v}.",
  "track.coroutines.m.race.ev.write": "{who} writes the new value",
  "track.coroutines.m.race.s.writeLost.title": "{who} overwrites, one increment is lost",
  "track.coroutines.m.race.s.writeLost.explain": "Between {who} reading and writing, the other side wrote; {who} overwrites with the old value plus 1 ({v}) and one increment is lost.",
  "track.coroutines.m.race.ev.writeLost": "{who} overwrites, one increment is lost",
  "track.coroutines.m.race.s.waitLock.title": "{who} waits for the lock",
  "track.coroutines.m.race.s.waitLock.explain": "The Mutex is held by the other side, so {who} suspends (without blocking a thread) until the lock is released.",
  "track.coroutines.m.race.ev.waitLock": "{who} waits for the lock",
  "track.coroutines.m.race.s.gotLock.title": "{who} gets the lock",
  "track.coroutines.m.race.s.gotLock.explain": "The lock is released; {who} is resumed and holds the lock.",
  "track.coroutines.m.race.ev.gotLock": "{who} gets the lock",
  "track.coroutines.m.race.s.lock.title": "{who} takes the lock",
  "track.coroutines.m.race.s.lock.explain": "{who} takes the Mutex with `withLock` before reading.",
  "track.coroutines.m.race.ev.lock": "{who} takes the lock",
  "track.coroutines.m.race.s.unlock.title": "{who} releases the lock",
  "track.coroutines.m.race.s.unlock.explain": "{who} finished writing and releases the lock; only then can the other side read.",
  "track.coroutines.m.race.ev.unlock": "{who} releases the lock",
  "track.coroutines.m.race.s.atomicInc.title": "{who} increments atomically",
  "track.coroutines.m.race.s.atomicInc.explain": "`incrementAndGet()` reads, adds and writes as one uninterruptible operation; the counter becomes {v}.",
  "track.coroutines.m.race.ev.atomicInc": "{who} increments atomically",
  "track.coroutines.m.race.s.finish.title": "{who} completes",
  "track.coroutines.m.race.s.finish.explain": "{who} has incremented 3 times.",
  "track.coroutines.m.race.ev.finish": "{who} completes",
  "track.coroutines.m.race.ch.lost": "An increment from the other side is overwritten",
  "track.coroutines.m.race.co.try": "Run \"none\" with the \"alternate\" pattern, then switch to Mutex and compare the final result.",
  "track.coroutines.m.race.co.lost": "`volatile` does not solve this: it guarantees reads and writes of each variable but not atomicity of larger actions such as an increment. [[src:kt-shared-state]]",
  "track.coroutines.m.race.co.none": "With \"A finishes, then B\" the result is right only because nothing happened to interleave; unprotected code is not safe just because one run gave the right answer.",
  "track.coroutines.m.race.co.mutex": "`Mutex` is the coroutine alternative to `synchronized`/`ReentrantLock`; the key difference is that `lock()` suspends and does not block a thread. [[src:kt-shared-state]]",
  "track.coroutines.m.race.co.confine": "Confining all access to one thread (thread confinement) is typically used in UI apps; with no suspension point between the read and the write, nothing can interleave. [[src:kt-shared-state]]",
  "track.coroutines.m.race.co.atomic": "Thread-safe data structures are the general solution for both threads and coroutines, but do not easily scale to complex state or operations. No single approach is best for every case: choose by the invariant to keep and the amount of contention. [[src:kt-shared-state]]",
  "track.coroutines.m.vtime.title": "runTest and virtual time",
  "track.coroutines.m.vtime.overview": "A test has two coroutines: A waits 1000 ms and B waits 2500 ms. The test does not wait for real but uses a virtual clock. You choose `advanceTimeBy` or `advanceUntilIdle`, and try switching to another dispatcher with `withContext`.",
  "track.coroutines.m.vtime.wrapup": "`runTest` controls virtual time through the `TestCoroutineScheduler`: delays are skipped and coroutines run when you advance the clock. `advanceTimeBy` runs the coroutines scheduled before that point; `advanceUntilIdle` runs all the remaining ones. Switching to another dispatcher stops delays from being skipped.",
  "track.coroutines.m.vtime.simp.1": "Only two coroutines with two fixed delays; `runCurrent` at the exact boundary time, multiple schedulers and `StandardTestDispatcher` versus `UnconfinedTestDispatcher` are not simulated.",
  "track.coroutines.m.vtime.ctl.hint": "Choose how to advance the virtual clock.",
  "track.coroutines.m.vtime.ctl.adv": "How to advance the clock",
  "track.coroutines.m.vtime.ctl.adv.by": "advanceTimeBy",
  "track.coroutines.m.vtime.ctl.adv.idle": "advanceUntilIdle",
  "track.coroutines.m.vtime.ctl.by": "advanceTimeBy amount",
