  "track.coroutines.a7.quiz.q4.q": "What about `stateIn(scope)` without an `initialValue`?",
  "track.coroutines.a7.quiz.q4.a": "It is a suspending function that waits until the upstream emits its first value.",
  "track.coroutines.a7.quiz.q4.why": "A StateFlow must always have a value.",
  "track.coroutines.a7.quiz.q5.q": "What is `WhileSubscribed()`'s default `stopTimeoutMillis`?",
  "track.coroutines.a7.quiz.q5.a": "0: stop as soon as the last listener leaves.",
  "track.coroutines.a7.quiz.q5.why": "And `replayExpirationMillis` defaults to `Long.MAX_VALUE` (keep the replay forever).",
  "track.coroutines.a9.quiz.q3.q": "Which scope do you pass to `shareIn`/`stateIn` inside `runTest`?",
  "track.coroutines.a9.quiz.q3.a": "`backgroundScope`.",
  "track.coroutines.a9.quiz.q3.why": "It is cancelled when the test ends, so `runTest` does not wait for the never-ending sharing coroutine.",
  "track.coroutines.a9.quiz.q4.q": "Why is `take(n).toList()` a weak way to test an infinite flow?",
  "track.coroutines.a9.quiz.q4.a": "It ignores everything after the n-th element.",
  "track.coroutines.a9.quiz.q4.why": "A wrong element at position n+1 goes unnoticed; Turbine's `expectNoEvents()` or a list collected in backgroundScope does better.",
  "track.coroutines.m.launchsusp.title": "A regular function launching on a scope, or a suspending function?",
  "track.coroutines.m.launchsusp.overview": "The caller (a coroutine in viewModelScope) calls `sendNotifications(n1, n2, n3)`; sending takes 1000, 2000, 1500 ms. Pick the kind of function and an incident, then press Step: when does the caller continue, where does the error go, how far does cancellation reach?",
  "track.coroutines.m.launchsusp.wrapup": "Regular function + outer scope: returns at once, does not wait, errors are handled by that scope, and sending is NOT cancelled with the caller (it belongs to the other scope). Suspending function + supervisorScope: the caller waits until every child is done, one child's error does not take the others down, and cancelling the caller cancels the sends still running. Given the choice, prefer the suspending function: it is easier to control and synchronize.",
  "track.coroutines.m.launchsusp.simp.1": "Send durations, the failure time (800 ms) and the cancellation time (1200 ms) are the site's assumptions. Behaviour follows Marcin Moskała's comparison of launching coroutines with suspending functions, and the `supervisorScope` docs.",
